(Abstract) Tj T*
/F2 10 Tf
14 TL
(BackgroundThe relation between sodium intake and cardiovascular disease remains controversial, ) Tj T*
(owing in part to inaccurate assessment of sodium intake. Assessing 24-hour urinary excretion ) Tj T*
(over a period of multiple days is considered to be an accurate method.MethodsWe included ) Tj T*
(individual-participant data from six prospective cohorts of generally healthy adults; sodium ) Tj T*
(and potassium excretion was assessed with the use of at least two 24-hour urine samples per ) Tj T*
(participant. The primary outcome was a cardiovascular event \(coronary revascularization or ) Tj T*
(fatal or nonfatal myocardial infarction or stroke\). We analyzed each cohort using consistent ) Tj T*
(methods and combined the results using a random-effects meta-analysis.ResultsAmong 10,709 ) Tj T*
(participants, who had a mean \(+/-SD\) age of 51.5+/-12.6 years and of whom 54.2% were women, ) Tj T*
(571 cardiovascular events were ascertained during a median study follow-up of 8.8 years ) Tj T*
(\(incidence rate, 5.9 per 1000 person-years\). The median 24-hour urinary sodium excretion was ) Tj T*
(3270 mg \(10th to 90th percentile, 2099 to 4899\). Higher sodium excretion, lower potassium ) Tj T*
(excretion, and a higher sodium-to-potassium ratio were all associated with a higher ) Tj T*
(cardiovascular risk in analyses that were controlled for confounding factors \(P<=0.005 for all ) Tj T*
(comparisons\). In analyses that compared quartile 4 of the urinary biomarker \(highest\) with ) Tj T*
(quartile 1 \(lowest\), the hazard ratios were 1.60 \(95% confidence interval [CI], 1.19 to 2.14\) ) Tj T*
(for sodium excretion, 0.69 \(95% CI, 0.51 to 0.91\) for potassium excretion, and 1.62 \(95% CI, ) Tj T*
(1.25 to 2.10\) for the sodium-to-potassium ratio. Each daily increment of 1000 mg in sodium ) Tj T*
(excretion was associated with an 18% increase in cardiovascular risk \(hazard ratio, 1.18; 95% ) Tj T*
(CI, 1.08 to 1.29\), and each daily increment of 1000 mg in potassium excretion was associated ) Tj T*
(with an 18% decrease in risk \(hazard ratio, 0.82; 95% CI, 0.72 to 0.94\).ConclusionsHigher ) Tj T*
(sodium and lower potassium intakes, as measured in multiple 24-hour urine samples, were ) Tj T*
(associated in a dose-response manner with a higher cardiovascular risk. These findings may ) Tj T*
(support reducing sodium intake and increasing potassium intake from current levels. \(Funded by ) Tj T*
(the American Heart Association and the National Institutes of Health.\).) Tj T*
